  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,5]?$",
  "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,5]$ and differentiable on $(-1,5).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,5]$ and differentiable on $(-1,5)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,5].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$  is continuous on $[-1,5]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=2.$  Hence, $g(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,5).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, $q(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,5].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,5]$ but has corner points at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ Hence, $r(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,5).$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.",
